# Security Rules

## Authentication

Use Django's built-in authentication system.

Never create custom password hashing or authentication mechanisms.

Never store plaintext passwords.

## Authorization

Every protected view must require authentication.

Every Note query that exposes user data must be restricted to the authenticated user.

Never fetch a Note only by its primary key when the result will be shown to a user.

Use ownership filtering such as:

get_object_or_404(Note, pk=pk, owner=request.user)

## Note Creation

Never trust a client-supplied owner or user field.

The owner must always be assigned server-side from:

request.user

## Forms

Every POST form must use Django CSRF protection.

Do not disable CSRF protection.

## Security Changes

Do not weaken Django security mechanisms simply to make development easier.

If a security-related change is necessary, explain why before implementing it.